<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramappa Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1213">1213</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Rudreswara Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ramappa Temple, or Rudreswara Temple, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple at Palampet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built in 1213 by Recherla Rudra, a general of the Kakatiya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganapati-kakatiya">Ganapatideva</a>. Named after its chief sculptor, it is famed for its finely carved basalt brackets and dancing figures and its light floating bricks. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2021.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramappa-temple.jpg" alt="Ramappa Temple" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nirav Lad · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramappa_Temple_(Human_Scale).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
